        "label": "Keyword retrieval with BM25"
      },
      "stableKey": "step#1"
    },
    {
      "id": "n2",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "Keyword retrieval scores a passage by the query terms it contains, weighted so that rare terms count for more than common ones. BM25 is the usual scoring function for this; it also dampens the effect of a term repeated many times and corrects for passage length. BM25 needs no training and is cheap to run, which is why it remains the baseline. Its strength is exact matching. A query for the error code `E-4012` or the setting `sync.retry_window` finds the passages that contain those strings, even when they appear only once in the whole collection. Its weakness is vocabulary mismatch: a user who asks \"why does my upload stall\" will not match a passage that only says \"transfers pause when the connection drops\"."
      },
      "explained_by": "n1"
    },
    {
      "id": "n3",
      "type": "shape",
      "content": {
        "shape": "rectangle",
        "label": "Vector retrieval with embeddings"
      },
      "stableKey": "step#2"
    },
    {
      "id": "n4",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "Vector retrieval turns the query and every passage into embeddings, lists of numbers produced by an encoder model, and returns the passages whose embeddings lie closest to the query's. Passages that mean the same thing end up close together even when they share no words, so the stalled-upload question can find the passage about paused transfers. Embeddings are weaker on exact codes and identifiers. To the encoder, `E-4012` and `E-4021` look almost the same, and a rare product name may be split into fragments that carry little meaning. In our tests, vector retrieval alone missed the correct passage for 31 of 50 queries that consisted mainly of an error code. Embeddings must also be recomputed whenever the encoder changes, which for this collection takes about 6 hours."
      },
      "explained_by": "n3"
    },
    {
      "id": "n5",
      "type": "shape",
      "content": {
        "shape": "rectangle",
        "label": "Candidate generation (Hybrid Search)"
      },
      "stableKey": "step#3"
    },
    {
      "id": "n6",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "Hybrid search runs both retrievers on the same query and merges their result lists. Hybrid search can improve recall when lexical and semantic signals are complementary, that is, when each retriever finds relevant passages that the other misses. If both retrievers tend to return the same passages, or if one of them is poorly tuned for the collection, merging adds latency without adding much. BM25 scores and vector similarities sit on different scales, so they cannot simply be added together. We use Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF), which ignores the raw scores and uses only the rank of each passage in each list. A passage receives `1/(k + rank)` from every list it appears in, and the contributions are summed. We set `k = 60`, a common default; a larger k flattens the difference between the top ranks and the lower ones."
      },
      "explained_by": "n5"
    },
    {
      "id": "n7",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "Each retriever returns its top 50 passages, so the fused list holds at most 100 candidates, and usually fewer because some passages appear in both lists. This stage is tuned for recall rather than precision: the goal is to make sure the right passage is somewhere in the list, not that it comes first. Raising the limit to 100 per retriever put the right passage in the list slightly more often, but it roughly doubled the time spent reranking, so we kept 50."
      },
      "explained_by": "n5"
    },
    {
      "id": "n8",
      "type": "shape",
      "content": {
        "shape": "rectangle",
        "label": "Reranking"
      },
      "stableKey": "step#4"
    },
    {
      "id": "n9",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "A reranker then rescores the fused candidates. We use a cross-encoder, a model that reads the query and one passage together and outputs a single relevance score. Because it sees both texts at once, it judges relevance far more accurately than either retriever. That accuracy is expensive, so the cross-encoder runs only on the fused candidates, never on the whole collection. After reranking we keep the top 8 passages."
      },
      "explained_by": "n8"
    },
    {
      "id": "n10",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "Reranking improves the precision of the top results, but it does not guarantee that the final answer is correct. The reranker can only reorder what retrieval found: if the right passage is not among the candidates, reranking cannot recover it. A passage can also be highly relevant and still out of date, which no relevance score will detect."
      },
      "explained_by": "n8"
    },
    {
      "id": "n11",
      "type": "shape",
      "content": {
        "shape": "rectangle",
        "label": "Generation"
      },
      "stableKey": "step#5"
    },
    {
      "id": "n12",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "The language model receives the question and the top 8 passages, and is instructed to answer only from them and to cite the passages it used. If none of the passages addresses the question, it should say so rather than answer from its general knowledge. Citations let a reader check each claim against its passage, but they do not show whether the passage itself is current. For example, asked \"What does E-4012 mean?\", the assistant retrieves the error-code table through BM25 and the article on resumable uploads through vector search, and the reranker keeps both in the top 8, so the answer can define the code and explain the fix."
      },
      "explained_by": "n11"
    },
    {
      "id": "a1",
      "type": "text",
      "origin": "analysis",
      "title": "Overall Process",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "This flow describes a hybrid search system that combines keyword and vector retrieval, reranking, and a language model for answer generation. The goal is to leverage the strengths of both retrieval methods to improve recall and then refine precision with a reranker before generating a final answer."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "a2",
      "type": "text",
      "origin": "analysis",
      "title": "Key Decisions/Trade-offs",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "- **Candidate Limit:** Keeping 50 passages per retriever (total 100 candidates) balances recall with reranking time.\n- **RRF k-value:** `k=60` is used for Reciprocal Rank Fusion, a common default that influences how top ranks are weighted.\n- **Reranker Scope:** The expensive cross-encoder is only run on the fused candidates, not the entire collection, to manage cost."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "a3",
      "type": "text",
      "origin": "analysis",
      "title": "Potential Limitations",
      "content": {
        "markdown": "- **Reranker Dependency:** If the correct passage isn't in the initial candidate list, the reranker cannot recover it.\n- **Passage Freshness:** Relevance scores do not detect outdated information, which could lead to incorrect answers from the language model.\n- **Embedding Recomputation:** Recomputing embeddings takes 6 hours, indicating a potential bottleneck for frequent encoder updates."
      }
    }
